title: IntroductionPart0
---
<<empty characterSprite>>
chip: this place is fucking miserable.
chip: you get up at the asscrack of dawn and work your tail off until the sun sets, and for what?
chip: you'll only ever be one measly little fish in a sea of people. or a number on some rich bastard's spreadsheet.

chip: nobody cares about who you are, or how you're doing, no, none of that. it's just about what you can do for them.
chip: and even if you do everything right, you STILL get fucked! 

narrator: chip kicks a rock while trudging down the sidewalk.

chip: ...
chip: god.
chip: this shit blows. 
chip: like, in the worst way.

chip: depressed, single, AND laid off... yeah, what more could a guy want?

narrator: he sighs as he pulls a sleek black smartphone out of his back pocket and enters his passcode.

<<playerPhone characterSprite>>
chip: but whatever. he said he was at 38 hemingway street... "my address... see you at 2". with a purple heart at the end. ha.

<<empty characterSprite>>
chip: hopefully this'll get my mind off of all that garbage from earlier.
chip: alright, that's 86 hemingway. 
chip: ... and that's 90. 
chip: ...
chip: ... ... ...
chip: shit, i'm totally going in the wrong direction.

===
title: IntroductionPart1
---
<<chipNeutral characterSprite>>
chip: 34... 36... 
chip: there we go, 38. finally. and he said the apartment number was...

<<empty characterSprite>>
narrator: he checks his phone again.

<<chipNeutral characterSprite>>
chip: 20. cool.

<<empty characterSprite>>
narrator: chip pushes the "20" button on the panel next to the door and within seconds, hears a buzz.

<<chipNeutral characterSprite>>
chip: damn, okay, that was fast. someone's desperate...

<<empty characterSprite>>
narrator: the front door of the apartment building unlocks with a nice, crisp click. chip pushes it open.

<<chipNeutral characterSprite>>
chip: oh thank god, it's warm in here.

<<empty characterSprite>>
narrator: looking around, he doesn't see anything particularly eyecatching. couple of taiga packages lying on the ground, dust piling up in the corners...
narrator: it's almost like looking at a palette-swapped copy of his own apartment building. except...

<<chipNeutral characterSprite>>
chip: what floor was he on again? i'm only seeing 1-5 on the doors down here, so logically speaking... it'd at least be the fifth floor.
chip: now the real question is, does this place have an elevator?

<<empty characterSprite>>
narrator: upon further investigation, chip discovers that there is not, in fact, an elevator. tough luck.

<< chipNeutral characterSprite>>
chip: fuck. fine, this better be worth it.

<<empty characterSprite>>
narrator: he begrudgingly ascends five flights of stairs and winds up face to face with a quaint wooden door marked "20". it's cracked open just a hair.
narrator: out of respect, chip decides to knock anyway.

<<chipNeutral characterSprite>>
chip: hey, luce? or, luke? not sure how you'd rather–

<<empty characterSprite>>
narrator: the door swings open, cutting him off mid-sentence.

<<luciGreeting characterSprite>>
lucifer: oh! hey! wow, i didn't think you'd get here so quickly. um, hi! haha! 

<<chipNeutral characterSprite>>
chip: hey–

<<luciGreeting characterSprite>>
lucifer: and i gueeeeess "luce" would be closer, but like, you can just call me luci. or lucifer. or whatever you want.

<<chipNeutral characterSprite>>
chip: cool. so... can i come in?

<<luciGreeting characterSprite>>
lucifer: oh yeah! make yourself at home. but, uh... i didn't really get the chance to clean up before you got here.
lucifer: hope that's alright. it's not thaaaat bad, just messy, haha! you know how it is sometimes. with work and stuff.

<<chipNeutral characterSprite>>
chip: mm, yeah, i hear you.


